## The short version

Within your new team, write a **one-page charter**: who's on the team, what roles you're starting with, how you'll communicate, how often you'll meet, how you'll make decisions, and what "pulling your weight" means here. **You start it together in class on {{ S11_DATE }}**, in the last block of the session, with the instructor in the room — answering the two questions teams normally avoid. You finish it at your **first team meeting** — the same meeting where you create the project's repository — and submit it by end of day **{{ S12_DAY }}, {{ S12_DATE }}**, then live by it through December.

---

## Why this assignment exists

Most team failures in project courses — and in industry — are not technical. They're the predictable frictions of unstated expectations: one person thinks "response within a day" is obvious, another thinks weekends are off-limits; one assumes decisions need consensus, another assumes the founder decides. A charter doesn't prevent disagreement; it makes the *rules for disagreement* explicit while everyone still likes each other, which is the cheapest moment to decide them.

The <course-link type="page" id="pg-working-agreements">course reading on working agreements</course-link> makes the general case; this is your instance of it. There's a founder lesson here too, and it's the same one from licensing and governance: **groups that intend to last write their norms down.** In Week 8 you'll do it for strangers, in CONTRIBUTING.md; this is the internal version — the norms among the people already inside. And there's a practical stake: at midpoint and end of term you'll evaluate each other confidentially, and those evaluations adjust individual grades on team artifacts. The charter is the shared standard those evaluations reference — "pulling your weight" should mean something you all agreed to, not something everyone privately defined differently.

---

## What to produce

Written in class on the printable worksheet — <file path="../../resources/Team-Charter-Worksheet.pdf" canvas_folder="Course Handouts" /> — then typed up as one page and submitted here. It covers:

**1. The project & the team.** Project name, members, the one-line rationale you converged on, and the **URL of the project's public repository** — created at the same meeting, following <course-link type="page" id="pg-starting-your-repository">Starting Your Project Repository</course-link>. List who was at the meeting where you finished the charter.

**2. Starting roles.** Not rigid job titles — starting *lean responsibilities*: who leads (the founder/maintainer), who owns what area first (frontend, data, infra…), and the expectation that roles flex as the work reveals itself.

**3. Communication norms.** Where the team talks (channel), expected response time (be realistic — "within 24h on weekdays" beats "always on"), and what's urgent enough to interrupt.

**4. Meeting cadence.** When you meet outside class, for how long, and what a meeting must produce (decisions and assignments, not vibes).

**5. Decision-making.** Who decides what: which calls the maintainer makes solo, which need the team, and what happens on a deadlock (a named tiebreaker beats a stalemate).

**6. "Pulling your weight."** Concretely: roughly what hours-per-week commitment (~7 outside class), what visible contribution looks like (issues taken, PRs opened, reviews done), and how the team will raise it early if someone's slipping — kindly, directly, before it festers.

---

## Requirements & constraints

- **One page.** A charter no one rereads is a charter that doesn't exist.
- **Everyone writes it together** — a charter drafted by one person is one person's assumptions with signatures. Finishing it outside class means *at a meeting*, not by one person typing it up.
- **Realistic promises only.** Every norm should survive the question "will we actually do this in Week 11?"
- **It's a living document:** amend it by team agreement if reality proves a norm wrong, and resubmit the amended version here. Amending is maturity, not failure.
- **It stays with the team, not in the repository.** A charter is an internal working agreement: promises among the people already inside, including what happens when one of them slips. Teams in industry keep documents like this on an internal wiki or shared drive, not in the public codebase — a public repository is forever, and amendments tend to follow a hard week. The parts a stranger needs — who maintains the project, how decisions get made, where the project talks — go public later, rewritten for outsiders, in your <course-link type="assignment" id="cp7">Infrastructure Package</course-link>.

---

## How it's graded

Completion-graded against four checks:

| Check | What it looks like |
|---|---|
| **Complete** | All six sections present and specific to *this* team |
| **Concrete** | Norms are checkable ("24h weekday response"), not vague ("communicate well") |
| **Realistic** | Commitments a student schedule can actually keep |
| **Shared** | All members listed as present at the meeting where it was finished |
| **Repository** | The project's public repository URL is in §1, and the repository exists under the project's GitHub organization |

Its real grading happens later: the charter is the reference standard for the **confidential peer evaluations** (midpoint and end of term) that adjust individual grades on team artifacts.

